Pre-trained Image Classifier for Dog Breed Identification

Date: 2026-08-13
Platform / Environment: AWS AI & ML / Udacity AI Programming with Python curriculum
Language: Python

What I Did

Completed a computer-vision project that uses pre-trained Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) architectures to classify images of pets.

I worked with three pre-trained models:

* VGG
* ResNet
* AlexNet

The project evaluates the models based on their ability to identify dogs, classify dog breeds, and distinguish dogs from non-dog images.

The project is divided into several Python modules, with check_images.py acting as the main pipeline that coordinates the classification and evaluation process.

What I Learned

This project gave me practical experience working with an existing machine-learning pipeline rather than building a neural network from scratch.

Key areas I practised included:

* Using pre-trained CNN models for image classification.
* Working with transfer learning concepts and ImageNet-trained models.
* Parsing image filenames to extract ground-truth labels.
* Using Python dictionaries and lists to organise classification data.
* Using argparse to accept command-line arguments.
* Splitting a larger program into separate Python modules with specific responsibilities.
* Comparing model accuracy and execution time.
* Generating statistical results to evaluate classification performance.

Project Pipeline

The application follows this general process:

1. Parse command-line arguments for the image directory, CNN architecture and dog-name reference file.
2. Extract ground-truth pet labels from image filenames.
3. Run images through the selected pre-trained CNN.
4. Compare the predicted labels against the known labels.
5. Determine whether the actual and predicted labels represent dogs or non-dogs.
6. Calculate classification statistics.
7. Print the results and identify misclassifications.

Key Takeaways

One of the main things I took from this project was how much work surrounds a machine-learning model itself.

The CNN performs the classification, but the overall application also needs input handling, data preparation, label processing, evaluation and reporting.

I also saw the trade-off between model performance and execution time when comparing different pre-trained architectures.

Practical Relevance

This project helped me practise Python in a more structured application rather than only working with isolated exercises.

It also introduced me to practical computer-vision workflows and showed how pre-trained models can be incorporated into applications without training a neural network from scratch.

